
01 — General Information	UPDATED	What is the project name, and what is the primary business function this script performs?	Answered		State what business process this script automates or supports. 'Automation' alone is not sufficient.
01 — General Information	RETAINED	Is this a Standalone Script or a Bound Script? If Bound, which file type (Sheet, Doc, Form, Slide)?	Answered		Bound scripts have implicit access to the host file and should use @OnlyCurrentDoc unless broader access is justified.
01 — General Information	NEW	What is the deployment type: Web App, Add-on, API Executable, or Internal-only? Who is the intended user audience?	Answered		Deployment type determines attack surface. A public Web App has far greater exposure than an internal script.
01 — General Information	UPDATED	Who is the named owner and maintainer responsible for this script's security posture after deployment?	Answered		There must be one accountable person. Shared ownership without a named individual is not acceptable.
01 — General Information	NEW	Was any part of this script written, generated, or modified using an AI tool (Gemini, Claude, Copilot)? If yes, which tool and which parts?	Answered		If Yes, all AI-RISK questions are mandatory. Answering No when AI was used is a policy violation.
02 — Script Execution Model	NEW	Is the script configured to 'Execute as Me (Deployer)' or 'Execute as User'? State and justify the choice.	Answered		'Execute as Me' means every user who triggers the script runs with the deployer's full permissions. Choosing this because it was the default is not a justification.
02 — Script Execution Model	NEW	If 'Execute as Me': what is the deployer's account type (personal/service account) and what Google Workspace permissions does that account hold?	Answered		A script deployed under a Workspace Admin account effectively grants admin-level access to anyone who triggers it.
02 — Script Execution Model	NEW	If 'Execute as User': have users been informed and consented that the script accesses Google services using their credentials?	Answered		Undisclosed credential use can constitute unauthorized access. Consent must be explicit and documented.
02 — Script Execution Model	NEW	What access level is set: Only Myself, Anyone within the Organization, or Anyone (public)? Justify.	Answered		'Anyone' without authentication means a publicly callable endpoint. Default to 'Anyone within the Organization' at minimum.
03 — Secret Handling	UPDATED	List every secret this script uses: API keys, OAuth tokens, passwords, service account credentials, internal endpoints. State what each grants access to.	Answered		Vague answers like 'some API keys' will be rejected. List each individually so AppSec can assess blast radius.
03 — Secret Handling	UPDATED	For each secret: where exactly is it stored? Accepted: Google Cloud Secret Manager, Script/User Properties (restricted access), or runtime prompt.	Answered		Secrets in plain text comments, hard-coded strings, or shared documents are not accepted.
03 — Secret Handling	UPDATED	Who has edit access to the script project? Edit access = access to every secret stored in Script Properties. List names and roles.	Answered		If more than 3 people have edit access, justify why. Treat this list as a list of everyone who can read your secrets.
03 — Secret Handling	NEW	What is the rotation schedule for each secret? When was each last rotated? Who is responsible?	Answered		Secrets never rotated are a persistent risk. AppSec will require rotation before sign-off if no rotation has occurred.
03 — Secret Handling	NEW	Are any strings in the script source that resemble secrets (tokens, base64 blobs, 'key=', 'Bearer', 'AIza', 'sk-' patterns)? Have you searched?	Answered		Search the script for: strings >20 chars that are not user-facing text, any string matching credential patterns.
03 — Secret Handling	AI-RISK	If AI generated any part of the script: did you inspect every string literal in the generated code for placeholder credentials or hard-coded values left by the AI?	Answered		AI models frequently insert example credentials and sample tokens. You must have verified none exist in the final code.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	UPDATED	List every OAuth scope declared or implicitly used. Paste exact scope strings from appsscript.json.	Answered		Do not paraphrase scopes. Paste them verbatim. If unsure, check the OAuth consent screen or appsscript.json directly.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	UPDATED	For each scope: write one sentence explaining the specific functionality that requires it. If you cannot explain it, it must be removed.	Answered		AppSec will reject any scope without a written justification. 'The AI added it' is not a justification.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	UPDATED	Does the script use any broad/full-access scopes (/auth/drive, /auth/spreadsheets, /auth/gmail.modify)? Justify why a narrower scope cannot be used.	Answered		/auth/spreadsheets.readonly suffices for read-only. /auth/drive.file limits to files the script created. Narrow first, widen only if necessary.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	RETAINED	Are all scopes explicitly declared in appsscript.json, or are some implicitly granted? How was this verified?	Answered		Implicit scopes are granted based on what APIs you call. Explicit declaration prevents scope creep.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	RETAINED	Is @OnlyCurrentDoc used? If the script is bound to one document and only needs that document, @OnlyCurrentDoc is required.	Answered		Without it, a bound script requests access to all documents of that type the user can access.
04 — Scopes & Permissions	AI-RISK	If generated by AI: did you audit all scopes in the generated appsscript.json line-by-line? What scopes did the AI request that you removed?	Answered		AI tools routinely request full Drive, Spreadsheets, and Gmail scopes for scripts needing only narrow access. Document what you reduced.
05 — Data Handling	NEW	What data does this script read and write? State sources (which Sheets, Docs, APIs, user inputs) and destinations.	Answered		Describe the full data flow. If reading from a sheet, state which columns. If writing to Drive, state folder and output file permissions.
05 — Data Handling	NEW	Does the script process PII (names, emails, employee IDs, phone numbers, customer data)? If yes, is there a data handling approval on file?	Answered		PII processing requires explicit sign-off. State what PII is collected, why necessary, and confirm compliance with org data policy.
05 — Data Handling	NEW	Does the script send data to external services outside Google Workspace? List every external destination, what data is sent, and how it is authenticated.	Answered		Any data leaving Google Workspace must be reviewed. Destinations must be on the approved vendor list.
05 — Data Handling	NEW	Are any internal URLs, GCP project IDs, API endpoints, or environment identifiers hard-coded in the script? List them.	Answered		Hard-coded internal identifiers expose infrastructure details if the script is shared. Store in Script Properties instead.
05 — Data Handling	NEW	How does the script validate inputs before processing? What happens with malformed, empty, or malicious input?	Answered		Scripts passing unvalidated user input to SpreadsheetApp.getRange(), UrlFetchApp, or eval() are vulnerable. Describe your validation logic.
06 — Logging & Audit	NEW	Does the script produce logs? Where are they stored — Apps Script execution logs, Cloud Logging, a Sheet, or elsewhere?	Answered		If no logs exist at all, explain how you would detect unexpected behavior in production.
06 — Logging & Audit	NEW	What specific events does the script log? For each: what is captured, why, and what is the minimum data needed?	Answered		Logs must be purposeful. Logging the full API response body is often dangerous. Each log statement needs a reason.
06 — Logging & Audit	NEW	Confirm explicitly: do any log statements capture secrets, tokens, passwords, PII, or full API response bodies? How was this verified?	Answered		This is a common AI-generated code failure — the model logs full request/response objects for debugging. Read every Logger.log() call.
06 — Logging & Audit	NEW	Who can view the script's logs? Are logs access-controlled, or visible to anyone with access to the log destination?	Answered		A log Sheet shared with the entire org, or logs viewable by all script editors, may expose operational details.
06 — Logging & Audit	UPDATED	How frequently are logs reviewed? Who reviews them? What would trigger an alert based on log content?	Answered		Original asked 'are logs reviewed?' — this is too vague. A monthly review by a named person with documented anomaly criteria is expected.
07 — Third-Party Libraries	NEW	Does this script use any third-party libraries? If yes: name, Script ID, version, and purpose for each.	Answered		Check the script editor Libraries section. If none, state explicitly. Leaving blank is not acceptable.
07 — Third-Party Libraries	NEW	For each library: has it been reviewed for suspicious scope requests, obfuscated code, or known issues? Where was it sourced?	Answered		Libraries from Stack Overflow or AI suggestions without independent verification are a risk. Verify each Script ID against official documentation.
07 — Third-Party Libraries	NEW	Is each library pinned to a specific version, or set to auto-update? What is the risk of auto-updating?	Answered		Auto-updating libraries can introduce breaking changes or malicious updates. Production scripts should be pinned.
07 — Third-Party Libraries	AI-RISK	If AI suggested a library by name or Script ID: did you independently verify it before adding? Describe your verification.	Answered		AI tools can hallucinate library Script IDs or suggest abandoned/compromised libraries. Never trust an AI-provided Script ID without independent verification.
08 — Triggers & Automation	UPDATED	List every trigger: type (time-based, onEdit, onFormSubmit, installable), frequency or condition, and what function it executes.	Answered		If no triggers, state explicitly. Include the exact function name — not just 'a daily trigger that runs the script.'
08 — Triggers & Automation	NEW	Under whose Google account are the triggers installed? Is this the deployer account or a different user's account?	Answered		Installable triggers run with the credentials of whoever installed them. A trigger installed by an admin runs as that admin.
08 — Triggers & Automation	RETAINED	If a trigger fires with stale, corrupted, or maliciously modified input data: what is the worst-case outcome?	Answered		Could tampered data cause unintended email sends? Export data to an attacker-controlled URL? Write incorrect values affecting business decisions?
08 — Triggers & Automation	NEW	Is there a procedure to disable or pause all triggers in an emergency? Who has access and how long would it take?	Answered		If only the original developer knows how to stop triggers, that is a risk. Emergency disable must be documented and executable by at least two people.
08 — Triggers & Automation	UPDATED	Are trigger executions logged? How would you know if a trigger fired unexpectedly or with unexpected parameters?	Answered		Each trigger execution should write a timestamped log entry. 'We would notice eventually' is not an acceptable answer.
09 — AI-Assisted Development	AI-RISK	What AI tool(s) were used, for which parts, and what were the prompts? Were internal details, credentials, or business data included in prompts?	Answered		Prompts containing internal data may be processed by the AI provider's infrastructure. This must be reported to AppSec.
09 — AI-Assisted Development	AI-RISK	Did you read every line of the AI-generated code before deploying? Can you explain what every function does?	Answered		'The AI wrote it and it worked when tested' is not code ownership. You must be able to explain it to an AppSec reviewer.
09 — AI-Assisted Development	AI-RISK	Was the AI-generated code tested in a non-production sandbox before deployment? What test cases were run?	Answered		Testing only in production is not acceptable. Describe inputs tested, edge cases, and error conditions.
09 — AI-Assisted Development	AI-RISK	Did the AI suggest a deployment configuration (execution identity, access level, triggers)? Did you independently verify it or accept it without review?	Answered		AI defaults almost always choose 'Execute as Me' and broad access levels because they produce fewer runtime errors. These are rarely the correct security choice.
09 — AI-Assisted Development	AI-RISK	If AI was used to update an existing script: what specifically changed? Was a diff reviewed before redeployment?	Answered		AI edits can silently introduce new API calls, additional scopes, or changed data handling. A line-by-line diff must be reviewed.
10 — Script Access & Trust	UPDATED	List every individual with edit access: name, role, and business reason for edit access.	Answered		Edit access = access to Script Properties = access to every stored secret. Every editor is effectively a secret holder.
10 — Script Access & Trust	UPDATED	Are any external contractors or vendors listed as editors? Vetting status, who approved, and when does access expire?	Answered		Contractor access should be time-bound and revoked at contract end. Permanent external editor access is not acceptable.
10 — Script Access & Trust	NEW	What is the process when a team member with edit access leaves or changes roles? How quickly is access removed, and who is responsible?	Answered		Stale access to scripts with stored secrets is a persistent credential exposure risk. The removal process must be documented.
10 — Script Access & Trust	NEW	Is the project sharing set to 'Restricted' (named editors only) or 'Anyone with the link can edit/view'? Confirm current setting.	Answered		'Anyone with the link can view' exposes the code. 'Anyone can edit' exposes code and all stored secrets. Must be Restricted for sensitive scripts.
11 — UI & Integration	RETAINED	Does the script present any UI — custom menus, sidebars, dialogs, HTML service pages? Describe each element and its purpose.	Answered		UI elements that request user input must be assessed for what they collect and where that data goes.
11 — UI & Integration	UPDATED	Does any UI element prompt users to input a secret, token, or credential? If yes, how is the input handled after collection?	Answered		Credentials collected via UI must be used immediately and never logged, stored in a Sheet, or transmitted in plaintext.
11 — UI & Integration	UPDATED	List every external API or service this script connects to: service name, endpoint URL, data sent, and authentication method.	Answered		Every external call must be documented. Undisclosed external connections discovered during review will result in deployment rejection.
11 — UI & Integration	NEW	If using HTMLService: does the HTML contain inline JavaScript, eval(), or user-controlled content rendered without escaping?	Answered		HTMLService can be vulnerable to XSS if user input is rendered unescaped. Any innerHTML with unescaped user data must be flagged.
12 — Risk & Incident Response	UPDATED	If the most sensitive secret used by this script were leaked today: what systems, data, or users would be affected? What is the blast radius?	Answered		'It would be bad' is not an answer. State what the secret grants access to, how many users/records affected, and whether it could pivot to other systems.
12 — Risk & Incident Response	NEW	If the execution identity were compromised: what could an attacker do with it? What is the worst-case scenario?	Answered		What Google Workspace services can the deployer access? What data could be exported? What actions could be taken?
12 — Risk & Incident Response	NEW	Who is the first point of contact if a security issue is found? What is the escalation path to AppSec?	Answered		Expected path: Script Owner → AppSec (Priyansh) → Sr. Manager AppSec (Rajkumar Yadav) → Director Cloud Engineering (Paras Arora).
12 — Risk & Incident Response	NEW	Can this script be disabled in under 15 minutes without causing a critical business process failure? If not, what is affected?	Answered		Scripts that cannot be quickly disabled represent a containment risk. Fallback procedures must exist for business-critical scripts.
13 — Additional Notes	UPDATED	Is there any architectural decision, known technical debt, accepted risk, or security concern AppSec should know before review?	Answered		Flag anything unusual. Undisclosed known risks discovered during review will result in escalation.
13 — Additional Notes	NEW	Declaration: all answers are accurate and complete, no material information has been withheld. State your name and date.	Answered		This is a formal declaration. False or incomplete answers are a policy violation.
